#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# show

#% text
# `show(g1, g2, ...)` is one graphic with the content of the graphics g1, g2, ... drawn in order.

#% text
# `show(g1, g2, ..., options...)` combines the graphics and gives them these options.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Options given to `show` come first, then those of g1, g2, ... in order; when an option is given more than once the first value counts.

#% item
# A list of graphics counts as its elements: `show([g1, g2])` is `show(g1, g2)`.

#% item
# Directives inside one graphic do not reach into the next: each graphic's content stays a list of its own.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A disk and a circle drawn together:

#% cell
show(graphics([orange, disk()]), graphics([black, circle([0, 0], 1.2)]))
#% output
#= image("show.files/show-1.svgz")

#% text
# Options of the first graphic carry over:

#% cell
show(graphics(point([1, 1]), axes: true), graphics(line([[0, 0], [2, 2]])))
#% output
#= image("show.files/show-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# An option given to `show` wins over the graphics' own:

#% cell
show(graphics(disk(), frame: true), graphics(circle([2, 0], 1)), frame: false, plot_label: "two shapes")
#% output
#= image("show.files/show-3.svgz")

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Three-dimensional graphics combine with 3D plots:

#% cell
show(plot3d(x*y, [x, -1, 1], [y, -1, 1]), graphics3d([red, point_size(0.03), point([0, 0, 1])]))
#% output
#= image("show.files/show-4.svgz")

#% text
# Points placed at exact values (here a root found by `solve`) are made numbers:

#% cell
show(plot(x^3 - 2, [x, 0, 2]), graphics([red, point_size(0.02), point([x /. first(solve(x^3 == 2, x, reals)), 0])]))
#% output
#= image("show.files/show-5.svgz")

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Primitives and regions are shown as graphics of their own (a disk here):

#% cell
show(graphics(disk()), disk([2, 0], 1))
#% output
#= image("show.files/show-6.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics`, `plot`
